{
  "narrative_body": "Lithuania signed a €6.375 billion EU defense loan — the largest military financing deal in its history — days after drones from Russian airspace exploded over neighboring Latvia and Lithuanian jets scrambled to fill the gap in the sky.\n\nThe Security Action for Europe loan, signed May 9 by Finance Minister Kristupas Vaitiekūnas and Defense Minister Robertas Kaunas alongside two EU commissioners, locks in 45-year funding at EU borrowing rates with principal deferred for the first decade. A first advance of €956 million arrives within three months. The money is earmarked for a new land forces division, heavy armored vehicles, Baltic Defense Line fortifications, and — the largest single share, the prime minister said — air defense. The same week, Lithuania formally received its first M142 HIMARS rocket launchers at a ceremony in Camden, Arkansas, making it the 14th NATO country to field the system. Lockheed Martin simultaneously established a legal presence in Lithuania to pursue joint manufacturing opportunities.\n\nThe timing was instructive. On May 7, multiple drones crossed from Russian airspace into Latvia; one crashed into an oil storage facility near Rēzekne, 40 kilometers from the border. Latvia's army commander admitted the country's radar had not registered the first drone at all. Lithuanian jets — French-piloted, flying NATO Baltic Air Policing from Šiauliai — were scrambled to patrol Latvian airspace. Mr Kaunas pressed the point afterward: \"Strengthening anti-drone defence in our region should be a particular emphasis [for NATO] ... this is where we have threats today, and they are not theoretical but real.\" Latvia's defense minister assessed the drones were probably Ukrainian munitions that had gone off course. That framing matters: the incident was a navigation failure, not a deliberate Russian strike — but the air defense gap it exposed is real regardless of cause.\n\nEven as it prepared to build up at home, Vilnius committed to operating further afield. Gitanas Nausėda, the president, and Inga Ruginienė, the prime minister, both publicly endorsed joining a coalition to reopen the Strait of Hormuz — the first time the two have aligned publicly on an out-of-area deployment. The day after their statements, the American president paused the US operation in the strait. Neither Lithuanian leader wavered. Mr Nausėda framed Lithuania's contribution as a demining and freedom-of-navigation mission and confirmed the State Defence Council will meet shortly; the Seimas must still grant a deployment mandate. Ms Ruginienė called reopening the strait \"a major European interest.\" Their willingness to hold the commitment regardless of Washington's posture signals that Lithuania is acting on its own strategic logic, not following the American lead.\n\nAt joint exercises in Kalvarija — Lithuania, Poland, and Portugal simulating the defense of the Suwałki Corridor — Mr Nausėda stood alongside Karol Nawrocki, the Polish president, on the 235th anniversary of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth Constitution and went further still, saying Lithuania is \"prepared to receive as many allies as we can accommodate",
  "other_stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Seimas strips ex-PM Paluckas of parliamentary immunity amid corruption investigation",
      "summary": "The Seimas voted to lift former Prime Minister Gintauto Paluckas's legal immunity, enabling prosecutors to pursue criminal charges related to alleged conflicts of interest in his family's business dealings. Paluckas said he intends to remain in the Seimas; PM Ruginienė said the decision is his alone; President Nausėda urged him to act more principally; LSDP chairman Sinkevičius said the party would not pressure him to leave the faction."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Lithuania signs €6.4 billion EU SAFE defense loan for military modernization",
      "summary": "Lithuania signed the European Security Action via Enablement (SAFE) loan agreement on May 9 in Vilnius, securing nearly €6.375 billion in long-term EU loans for defense. The funds will support full equipment of a new land forces division, heavy armored vehicles, and Baltic Defense Line fortifications. PM Ruginienė noted the largest share will go to air defense; the deal was signed by the Finance and Defense ministers alongside EU Commissioners Kubilius and Serafin."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Two railway derailments ruled technical failures; government orders critical infrastructure protection plan within two weeks",
      "summary": "After two train derailments on the same weekend, PM Ruginienė convened the National Security Commission and announced that investigations found no evidence of sabotage — causes were technical and human error. She ordered Lithuanian Railways (LTG) and the Transport Ministry to submit a detailed critical infrastructure protection plan within two weeks."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Nausėda and Ruginienė signal Lithuania ready to join Strait of Hormuz coalition",
      "summary": "Following a US invitation to join a coalition ensuring safe navigation in the Strait of Hormuz, President Nausėda said Lithuania's plans to contribute are unchanged regardless of shifting US actions in the region, and that the State Defense Council will meet shortly. PM Ruginienė described reopening the strait as a major European interest. Latvia also confirmed receiving the US invitation."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Landsbergis quoted internationally warning Putin may test NATO with limited escalation",
      "summary": "TS-LKD chairman Gabrielius Landsbergis was quoted in Politico and picked up by multiple international outlets, warning that Putin could 'escalate horizontally against another neighbor' to avoid humiliating Ukraine negotiations — with coverage appearing in English, Spanish, and Ukrainian media."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ruginienė attends EPC Summit in Armenia, holds sideline discussions",
      "summary": "PM Ruginienė traveled to Yerevan to participate in the 8th European Political Community Summit, engaging in panel discussions with European leaders on security, Russia's war in Ukraine, and the ECAD anti-narcotics coalition."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Banks revise down Lithuania 2026 GDP forecasts amid inflation and global uncertainty",
      "summary": "SEB Bank forecast 3.2% GDP growth and 5.7% inflation for Lithuania in 2026, while Swedbank trimmed its GDP forecast to 3.0% from 3.5% (still fastest in the Baltics). The Bank of Lithuania issued a wide minimum wage forecast range of 6–12% for next adjustment. An Estonian economist sparked debate by arguing Lithuania has not clearly overtaken Estonia despite popular claims."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ruginienė communication team controversy as new adviser hired and interview format debated",
      "summary": "Reports emerged that former Seimas Speaker Graužinienė's adviser Rosvaldas Gorbačiovas is set to join PM Ruginienė's communications team. Separately, a controversial interview with Ruginienė conducted by political commentator Rima Urbonaitė on Laisvės TV triggered a public debate about journalistic standards and the boundaries of political interviewing."
    }
  ]
}

